   141	## 4. Research decisions and reusable implementation references
   142	
   143	### 4.1 ACP foundation
   144	
   145	Use the official Rust SDK as the client transport:
   731	## 8. Structured/native-terminal ownership handoff
   732	
   733	### 8.1 State machine
   734	
   735	```text
   736	StructuredOwned
   737	  -> PreparingTerminal
   738	  -> TerminalOwned
   739	  -> PreparingStructured
   740	  -> StructuredOwned
   741	```
   742	
   743	`PreparingTerminal`:
   744	
   745	1. block new structured prompts;
   746	2. finish or interrupt the active turn according to user choice;
   747	3. flush normalized events and capture provider/native IDs;
   748	4. close/unsubscribe the structured writer without deleting the provider session;
   749	5. start the existing PTY with the exact native resume command;
   750	6. prove the new PTY owns the same native conversation;
   751	7. begin incremental transcript projection;
   752	8. mark terminal ownership only after the TUI is live.
   753	
   754	`PreparingStructured`:
   755	
   756	1. block PTY composer writes;
   757	2. ask the user to exit or explicitly terminate the native TUI when still active;
   758	3. stop transcript projection after the final append is processed;
   759	4. resume/load the provider session through ACP;
   760	5. import/reconcile turns added by the native TUI;
   761	6. prove native IDs and history boundary;
   762	7. acquire the structured writer lease;
   763	8. mark structured ownership.
   764	
   765	Failure rolls back to the previous usable owner. It never leaves two writers or loses the terminal scrollback.
   766	
   767	### 8.2 Fork to terminal/provider
   768	
   769	Separate actions:
   770	
   771	- **Open in native CLI** transfers the same conversation;
   772	- **Fork to native CLI** creates a new provider-native branch and leaves the structured source open;
   773	- **Fork with provider…** creates a new Assembly session with copied bounded context and a selected provider/model; it is not claimed as the same provider thread.
   774	
   775	### 8.3 Codex shared app-server optimization
   776	
   777	A later Codex-specific spike may connect both Assembly and the Codex TUI to one app-server daemon using the TUI’s remote endpoint support. This is an optimization only after proving:
   778	
   779	- one thread identity;
   780	- one active writer lease;
   781	- exact subscription/unsubscription behavior;
   782	- no duplicate rollout;
   783	- approvals and settings remain synchronized;
   784	- clean disconnect/reconnect.
   785	
   786	The first implementation does not depend on this optimization.
   787	
   788	---
   789	
   790	## 9. App-owned workflow orchestration
  1629	## 15. Persistence, recovery, security, and trust boundaries
  1630	
  1631	### 15.1 Sources of truth
  1632	
  1633	```text
  1634	Provider conversation/context
  1635	  Provider/ACP session storage and native transcript.
  1636	
  1637	Assembly normalized conversation journal
  1638	  Rebuildable projection/audit for UI continuity; not the provider context authority.
  1639	
  1640	Owned session/workspace state
  1641	  Existing versioned local persistence keyed by ownedId.
  1642	
  1643	Workflow runs
  1644	  Existing append-only orchestration ledger plus deterministic reducer.
  1645	
  1646	Usage analytics
  1647	  Append-only normalized usage events plus rebuildable aggregate cache.
  1648	```
  1649	
  1650	### 15.2 Recovery
  1651	
  1652	On app restart:
  1653	
  1654	1. reconcile owned sessions and surviving PTYs;
  1655	2. restore workspace snapshots without starting providers merely because rows exist;
  1656	3. reattach structured runtimes only for sessions that were active/running and whose provider supports resume/load;
  1657	4. restore terminal projections for surviving PTYs;
  1658	5. replay workflow ledger and mark indeterminate running nodes as recovering;
  1659	6. query provider session state before retrying work;
  1660	7. never rerun a possibly-completed consequential action without deterministic revalidation and user-visible outcome-unknown state.
  1661	
  1662	### 15.3 Secrets
  1663	
  1664	Never persist or expose through conversation/workflow/usage/browser telemetry:
  1665	
  1666	- provider tokens/API keys;
  1667	- cookies/local storage/form values;
  1668	- full process environments;
  1669	- GitHub auth headers/tokens;
  1670	- keychain values;
  1671	- arbitrary provider stderr containing secrets;
  1672	- complete remote HTML;
  1673	- unredacted credential-bearing URLs.
  1674	
  1675	### 15.4 Provider and sidecar trust
  1676	
  1677	- pinned version/hash;
  1678	- executable path shown in settings;
  1679	- bounded stderr ring;
  1680	- no Tauri IPC capability;
  1681	- explicit workspace roots;
  1682	- process group cleanup;
  1683	- update is a separate reviewed action;
  1684	- custom provider manifests require explicit trust and cannot silently inherit existing credentials.
  1685	
  1686	---
  1687	
  1688	## 16. Exact work packages
  1689	
