     1	/**
     2	 * The single terminal-IO owner for the /next shell.
     3	 *
     4	 * Everything that touches a PTY goes through here: ONE backend output listener
     5	 * for the whole app (not one per view), one `ownedId -> ptySessionId` map, and
     6	 * one manager instance holding the live views.
     7	 *
     8	 * Two rules this module exists to enforce:
     9	 *
    10	 * 1. **Exactly one listener.** The old shell registered a listener per terminal,
    11	 *    so output was written N times and every re-open leaked another
    12	 *    subscription. Here `attach()` is idempotent and routes by sessionId through
    13	 *    `manager.feedSession`, which writes to the view REGARDLESS of visibility —
    14	 *    that is what keeps background conversations live.
    15	 * 2. **`dispose()` never kills a PTY.** The old shell's
    16	 *    `disposeEmbeddedTerminal` closed the backend session on teardown, so a
    17	 *    dev-server reload murdered every running agent. Teardown here only
    18	 *    unsubscribes and drops views; `closeOwned` is the ONLY path that closes a
    19	 *    PTY.
